hints.content:
Notice the numerator is missing a $1^{\text{st}}$ degree term. Let's add it as $0x$.

$\begin{array}{r}
x\phantom{-5}
\\
x-2|\overline{x^2+0x-5}
\\
\mathllap{-(}\underline{x^2-2x\phantom{-5}\rlap )}
\\
2x-5
\end{array}$

options.explanation:
We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x-2$ to get $x^2+k$ (no $x$-term) where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x+2$ (remember the difference of squares pattern):

$\blueD{(x-2)(x+2)=x^2-4}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2-5}{x-2}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-4})-1}{x-2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-4}}{x-2}-\dfrac{1}{x-2}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x-2)(x+2)=x^2-4}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-4}}{x-2}-\dfrac{1}{x-2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x-2)}}\blueD{(x+2)}}{\cancel{x-2}}-\dfrac{1}{x-2}
\\\\
&=x+2-\dfrac{1}{x-2}
\end{align}$
